      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "This was in response to VRChat's announcement: [Where we’re landing on AI in VRChat](https://ask.vrchat.com/t/where-were-landing-on-ai-in-vrchat/49178/1).\n\nIn the end, I believe a *human-authored* label would be useless to potentially harmful.\n\nThe proposal to label *spam* is mostly a reflection of the path taken by Lobste.rs, and the issues they faced with user suggested AI labels:\n\n- https://lobste.rs/s/ggpwfi/it_s_enough_vibecoding_tag_crossed_from\n\n- https://lobste.rs/s/h0eate/updates_site_guidelines_ai_llm_tags\n\nAny sort of lack of \"human-authored\" tag or any \"AI\" tag is a tag of shame, used to shame or attack people. A spam flag shouldn't be.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2026-10-29T06:21:25.160Z",
  "deleteID": null,
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "I believe the binary stigmatic decision between “I used AI.” and “I’m a human.” is not the way to go. It's a tag of shame, used to direct unwanted behavior towards individuals.\n\nThe proposed likes / dislikes rating system for worlds can also be weaponized and gamed. I see the long-term harm of this system. Say an entirely *human-authored* world teaches in the world how to use AI to generate content for VRChat or discusses AI/LLM usage, I can see the potential of the likes / dislikes system being abused by people who *disagree* with the world’s content presented.\n\nA more appropriate solution would be to flag content for *spam*, which may include really low-quality content generated via AI/LLM tools but without significant human authorship. Content flagged for spam could be unlisted from search/discovery (or removed entirely), with options to appeal the flag.",
